DATA={'III/01': {'intro': 'Measure theory begins by specifying which sets are stable under countable operations and which size functions are countably additive. The resulting continuity properties are the set-theoretic engine behind integration.', 'sections': [('Sigma-algebras', "A sigma-algebra contains the empty set and is closed under complements and countable unions; De Morgan's laws give closure under countable intersections and differences."), ('Generated sigma-algebras', 'The sigma-algebra generated by a family is the intersection of all sigma-algebras containing it, giving the smallest measurable structure compatible with the generators.'), ('Borel sets', 'The Borel sigma-algebra is generated by open sets. On the real line, open intervals with rational endpoints already generate all Borel sets.'), ('Measures', 'A measure is a nonnegative countably additive set function with value zero at the empty set. Countable additivity forces monotonicity and subadditivity.'), ('Finite and sigma-finite measures', 'Finite measures have finite total mass; sigma-finite spaces are countable unions of finite-measure pieces and are the natural setting for product theory.'), ('Continuity from below', 'If measurable sets increase to a union, their measures increase to the measure of that union.'), ('Continuity from above', 'For decreasing measurable sets with finite measure at the first stage, measures decrease to the measure of the intersection.'), ('Completion and null sets', 'A complete measure space contains every subset of every measurable null set, so null-set modifications remain measurable.')], 'theorems': [('Continuity from below', 'If $E_n\\uparrow E$, then $\\mu(E_n)\\uparrow\\mu(E)$.', 'Disjointify the increase by writing $E$ as $E_1$ together with the successive differences $E_n\\setminus E_{n-1}$, then use countable additivity.'), ('Continuity from above', 'If $E_n\\downarrow E$ and $\\mu(E_1)<\\infty$, then $\\mu(E_n)\\downarrow\\mu(E)$.', 'Apply continuity from below to $E_1\\setminus E_n\\uparrow E_1\\setminus E$ and subtract from the finite quantity $\\mu(E_1)$.'), ('Countable subadditivity', 'For measurable $E_n$, $\\mu(\\cup_nE_n)\\le\\sum_n\\mu(E_n)$.', 'Replace the family by disjoint pieces $F_n=E_n\\setminus\\cup_{k<n}E_k$, use countable additivity, and compare $\\mu(F_n)\\le\\mu(E_n)$.')]}, 'III/02': {'intro': 'Measurable functions are those whose inverse images respect the measurable structure. Their closure under algebra, limits, and monotone simple approximation makes them the natural domain of Lebesgue integration.', 'sections': [('Inverse-image definition', 'A real-valued function is measurable when inverse images of Borel sets are measurable.'), ('Ray criteria', 'It is enough to test sets of the form $\\{f<a\\}$ or $\\{f>a\\}$ for all real $a$ because such rays generate the Borel sigma-algebra.'), ('Algebraic operations', 'Sums, products, absolute values, maxima, and minima of measurable real-valued functions remain measurable.'), ('Pointwise limits', 'Countable suprema, infima, limsup, liminf, and pointwise limits of measurable functions are measurable.'), ('Simple functions', 'A simple measurable function has finite range and measurable level sets; it is the finite-data model for integration.'), ('Approximation from below', 'Every nonnegative measurable function is the increasing pointwise limit of nonnegative simple functions obtained by dyadic quantization and truncation.'), ('Almost-everywhere equivalence', 'On complete measure spaces, changing a measurable function on a null set preserves measurability.'), ('Composition', 'Composing a measurable map with a continuous or Borel measurable scalar map preserves measurability.')], 'theorems': [('Pointwise limit theorem', 'A pointwise limit of measurable real-valued functions is measurable.', 'Represent the limit using limsup and liminf, each built from countable suprema and infima of measurable functions.'), ('Algebra closure', 'If $f$ and $g$ are measurable, then $f+g$ and $fg$ are measurable.', 'The map $x\\mapsto(f(x),g(x))$ is measurable into $\\mathbb R^2$, and addition and multiplication are continuous Borel maps.'), ('Simple approximation', 'If $f\\ge0$ is measurable, there exist simple $\\phi_n\\uparrow f$.', 'Truncate at height $n$ and replace values by the lower endpoints of dyadic intervals of width $2^{-n}$; measurability of the level sets gives simple functions and the mesh tends to zero.')]}, 'III/03': {'intro': 'The Lebesgue integral is constructed by integrating simple functions and taking monotone suprema. It is insensitive to null-set changes, linear on integrable functions, and controlled by the integral of the absolute value.', 'sections': [('Simple-function integral', 'For a nonnegative simple function $\\phi=\\sum a_k1_{E_k}$ on disjoint measurable level sets, define $\\int\\phi=\\sum a_k\\mu(E_k)$.'), ('Nonnegative functions', 'For measurable $f\\ge0$, define the integral as the supremum of simple-function integrals over $0\\le\\phi\\le f$.'), ('Positive and negative parts', 'Write $f=f^+-f^-$ with $f^+,f^-\\ge0$ and $|f|=f^++f^-$.'), ('Integrable functions', 'A measurable real-valued function is integrable when $\\int|f|<\\infty$, which rules out the indeterminate expression $\\infty-\\infty$.'), ('Monotonicity', 'If $0\\le f\\le g$, then $\\int f\\le\\int g$ because every simple lower approximation to $f$ is also one for $g$.'), ('Linearity', 'Integrable functions form a vector space and the integral is linear.'), ('Null-set invariance', 'If $f=g$ almost everywhere and one is integrable, then both represent the same integral.'), ('Integrals over sets', 'Define $\\int_Ef=\\int f1_E$, making restriction to measurable subsets part of the same formalism.')], 'theorems': [('Absolute integral inequality', 'For integrable $f$, $|\\int f|\\le\\int|f|$.', 'Use $-|f|\\le f\\le|f|$ and monotonicity of the integral.'), ('Zero integral criterion', 'If $f\\ge0$ and $\\int f=0$, then $f=0$ almost everywhere.', 'For $E_n=\\{f\\ge1/n\\}$, the inequality $\\int f\\ge(1/n)\\mu(E_n)$ forces every $E_n$ null; their union is $\\{f>0\\}$.'), ('Linearity', 'If $f,g$ are integrable and $a,b\\in\\mathbb R$, then $\\int(af+bg)=a\\int f+b\\int g$.', 'Prove first for nonnegative simple functions on a common refinement, pass to nonnegative measurable functions by monotone approximation, then use positive/negative parts.')]}, 'III/04': {'intro': "Monotone convergence, Fatou's lemma, and dominated convergence govern the passage of limits through integrals. Together they separate the roles of positivity, one-sided control, and integrable domination.", 'sections': [('Monotone convergence', 'Increasing nonnegative sequences allow direct interchange of pointwise limit and integral.'), ("Fatou's lemma", 'The integral of a nonnegative liminf cannot exceed the liminf of the integrals.'), ('Dominated convergence', 'Almost-everywhere convergence under a single integrable absolute bound permits limit-integral interchange for signed functions.'), ('Almost-everywhere convergence', 'Null exceptional sets do not affect the integral but still require measurable representatives.'), ('Convergence in measure', 'Error sets $\\{|f_n-f|>\\varepsilon\\}$ shrinking in measure define a weaker convergence mode.'), ('$L^1$ convergence', 'Norm convergence controls the integral of absolute error and implies convergence in measure.'), ('Series applications', 'MCT handles nonnegative series through increasing partial sums; DCT handles dominated signed series.'), ('Counterexamples', 'Moving spikes show that pointwise convergence alone does not control integrals.')], 'theorems': [('Monotone convergence theorem', 'If $0\\le f_n\\uparrow f$, then $\\int f_n\\uparrow\\int f$.', 'The integrals increase and are bounded above by $\\int f$; every simple function below $f$ is eventually captured up to an arbitrarily small factor on most of its level sets, forcing equality of the limit.'), ("Fatou's lemma", 'For nonnegative measurable $f_n$, $\\int\\liminf f_n\\le\\liminf\\int f_n$.', 'Set $g_N=\\inf_{n\\ge N}f_n$. Then $g_N\\uparrow\\liminf f_n$ and $\\int g_N\\le\\inf_{n\\ge N}\\int f_n$; apply MCT and let $N\\to\\infty$.'), ('Dominated convergence theorem', 'If $f_n\\to f$ a.e. and $|f_n|\\le g\\in L^1$, then $\\int f_n\\to\\int f$.', 'Apply Fatou to the nonnegative sequences $g\\pm f_n$ to obtain matching upper and lower bounds; also $|f|\\le g$ a.e., so $f$ is integrable.')]}, 'III/05': {'intro': 'Product measures combine measurable coordinate spaces, while Tonelli and Fubini convert multidimensional integration into iterated integration. Absolute integrability is the key condition that permits changing order safely.', 'sections': [('Product sigma-algebra', 'The product sigma-algebra is generated by measurable rectangles $A\\times B$.'), ('Product measures', 'On sigma-finite spaces, the product measure is characterized by $(\\mu\\times\\nu)(A\\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B)$ on rectangles.'), ('Sections of sets', 'For measurable $E\\subset X\\times Y$, the section $E_x=\\{y:(x,y)\\in E\\}$ is measurable for almost every relevant parameter under the standard product theory.'), ('Sections of functions', 'Fixing one variable in a measurable function produces measurable sections, allowing iterated integration.'), ('Tonelli theorem', 'Nonnegative measurable functions may be integrated in either order, with the common value possibly infinite.'), ('Fubini theorem', 'Absolutely integrable functions have integrable sections almost everywhere and finite iterated integrals equal to the product integral.'), ('Changing order', 'Absolute integrability prevents order-dependent cancellation.'), ('Applications', 'Areas, convolution kernels, expectations, and integral operators all rely on product integration.')], 'theorems': [('Tonelli theorem', 'For nonnegative measurable $f$ on a sigma-finite product space, the product integral equals both iterated integrals, possibly with value $\\infty$.', 'Prove first for rectangle indicators, then nonnegative simple functions, and finally pass to arbitrary nonnegative measurable functions by monotone convergence.'), ('Fubini theorem', 'If $f\\in L^1(\\mu\\times\\nu)$, then almost all sections are integrable and the iterated integrals equal $\\int f$.', 'Apply Tonelli to $|f|$ to obtain finite section integrals almost everywhere, then decompose $f$ into positive and negative parts.'), ('Rectangle rule', 'For measurable rectangles, $(\\mu\\times\\nu)(A\\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B)$.', 'This defines the canonical premeasure on rectangles; sigma-finiteness gives the standard extension and uniqueness on the generated product sigma-algebra.')]}, 'III/06': {'intro': '$L^p$ spaces turn integral size into geometry. After identifying functions equal almost everywhere, the $L^p$ quantities become genuine norms for $p\\ge1$, and the resulting spaces are complete.', 'sections': [('Almost-everywhere classes', 'Two measurable functions equal almost everywhere represent the same $L^p$ element.'), ('$L^p$ norms', 'For $1\\le p<\\infty$, use $\\|f\\|_p=(\\int|f|^p)^{1/p}$; for $p=\\infty$, use essential supremum.'), ('Essential supremum', 'The $L^\\infty$ norm ignores exceptional values on null sets and records the least almost-everywhere uniform bound.'), ('Vector-space structure', 'Hölder and Minkowski control sums and scalar multiples, making $L^p$ a normed vector space.'), ('Completeness', 'Every $L^p$ Cauchy sequence converges in $L^p$ for $1\\le p\\le\\infty$.'), ('Finite-measure inclusions', 'On finite-measure spaces, $L^q\\subset L^p$ when $q>p$, with an explicit measure factor.'), ('Convergence in measure', "For finite $p$, $L^p$ convergence implies convergence in measure by Chebyshev's inequality."), ('Approximation', 'Truncation, finite-measure cutoffs, and simple functions provide dense approximations in $L^p$ for $p<\\infty$.')], 'theorems': [('Norm-zero criterion', 'For $1\\le p\\le\\infty$, $\\|f\\|_p=0$ iff $f=0$ almost everywhere.', 'For finite $p$, $\\int|f|^p=0$ forces $|f|^p=0$ a.e.; for $p=\\infty$, essential supremum zero means $|f|=0$ a.e.'), ('Finite-measure inclusion', 'If $\\mu(X)<\\infty$ and $1\\le p<q\\le\\infty$, then $\\|f\\|_p\\le\\mu(X)^{1/p-1/q}\\|f\\|_q$.', 'For finite $q$, apply Hölder to $|f|^p\\cdot1$ with exponent $q/p$ and its conjugate; the $q=\\infty$ case follows directly.'), ('Completeness of $L^p$', 'For $1\\le p\\le\\infty$, $L^p$ is complete.', 'Extract a subsequence with summable norm increments, use the resulting pointwise absolutely summable majorant to construct an a.e. limit, prove subsequence norm convergence, and recover the full Cauchy sequence.')]}, 'III/07': {'intro': 'Hölder and Minkowski are the fundamental inequalities of $L^p$ geometry. Interpolation then explains how information at two exponents controls intermediate exponents.', 'sections': [('Conjugate exponents', 'For $1<p<\\infty$, the conjugate exponent $q$ satisfies $1/p+1/q=1$.'), ('Young inequality', 'The scalar bound $ab\\le a^p/p+b^q/q$ encodes the convex duality behind Hölder.'), ('Hölder inequality', 'Products of $L^p$ and $L^q$ functions lie in $L^1$ with sharp norm control.'), ('Minkowski inequality', 'The $L^p$ norm satisfies the triangle inequality.'), ('Equality cases', 'Sharpness is governed by proportionality of the powers $|f|^p$ and $|g|^q$.'), ('Discrete versions', 'Counting measure recovers the familiar finite-sum Hölder and Minkowski inequalities.'), ('Interpolation of norms', 'Intermediate $L^r$ norms are logarithmically convex between endpoint norms.'), ('Operator interpolation preview', 'Linear operators bounded at endpoint exponents often inherit intermediate bounds under an interpolation theorem.')], 'theorems': [('Hölder inequality', 'If $1<p,q<\\infty$ are conjugate, then $\\int|fg|\\le\\|f\\|_p\\|g\\|_q$.', "Normalize the two norms to one, apply Young's inequality pointwise to the normalized product, integrate, and rescale."), ('Minkowski inequality', 'For $1\\le p<\\infty$, $\\|f+g\\|_p\\le\\|f\\|_p+\\|g\\|_p$.', 'For $p>1$, estimate $\\int|f+g|^p$ by pairing $|f+g|^{p-1}$ with $|f|+|g|$ and apply Hölder; the case $p=1$ is immediate.'), ('Log-convex interpolation', 'If $1/r=\\theta/p+(1-\\theta)/q$, then $\\|f\\|_r\\le\\|f\\|_p^\\theta\\|f\\|_q^{1-\\theta}$.', 'Factor $|f|^r$ into suitable powers of $|f|^p$ and $|f|^q$ and apply Hölder with reciprocal weights.')]}, 'III/08': {'intro': 'Egorov, Vitali, and weak-$L^p$ theory compare convergence modes and exceptional-set control. They clarify how almost-everywhere convergence can become nearly uniform and how tail bounds encode borderline integrability.', 'sections': [('Convergence in measure', 'A sequence converges in measure when $\\mu(|f_n-f|>\\varepsilon)\\to0$ for every $\\varepsilon>0$.'), ('Egorov theorem', 'On finite-measure spaces, almost-everywhere convergence becomes uniform after removing a set of arbitrarily small measure.'), ('Uniform integrability', 'A family is uniformly integrable when integral mass cannot concentrate on sets of very small measure.'), ('Vitali convergence', 'Convergence in measure plus uniform integrability upgrades to $L^1$ convergence under the standard finite-measure hypotheses.'), ('Weak-$L^p$', 'Weak-$L^p$ controls tails through $\\sup_{t>0}t^p\\mu(|f|>t)$ rather than directly integrating $|f|^p$.'), ('Chebyshev inequality', 'Strong $L^p$ bounds imply weak-$L^p$ tail estimates.'), ('Subsequence extraction', 'Convergence in measure admits almost-everywhere convergent subsequences under standard assumptions.'), ('Borderline behavior', 'Power singularities often lie in weak-$L^p$ at the exact exponent where strong $L^p$ fails.')], 'theorems': [('Egorov theorem', 'If $\\mu(X)<\\infty$ and $f_n\\to f$ a.e., then for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is measurable $E$ with $\\mu(E)<\\varepsilon$ such that convergence is uniform on $X\\setminus E$.', 'For each accuracy level, form decreasing tail-exception sets. Their intersection is null; continuity from above on the finite-measure space yields a late stage with small measure, and a diagonal choice handles all accuracy levels.'), ('Chebyshev inequality', 'If $f\\in L^p$ and $t>0$, then $\\mu(|f|>t)\\le t^{-p}\\|f\\|_p^p$.', 'On the superlevel set, $|f|^p\\ge t^p$; integrate and rearrange.'), ('Vitali convergence principle', 'On a finite-measure space, convergence in measure plus uniform integrability implies $L^1$ convergence.', 'Split the error integral into the region where the error is small and its exceptional set; convergence in measure makes the exceptional set small and uniform integrability controls the mass there.')]}}
